cat > $B/BRIEF_steer.md <<'BRIEF'
You are the Builder on Peter Edwards' trading server, session 14. Read first: /root/pe-queue/reports/offering_prices.md, builder_handover_10.md, builder_handover_7.md (winners_check.md), goal_trade.md, books_and_goal_signal.md, and /root/pe-builder/BRIEF_league.md (HARD RULES unchanged: paper only, never an order endpoint, nothing deleted, back up before editing). Reuse session 10's quoter and its two fill models (queue and through-only) exactly; reuse session 7's feature builder (s7feat.py) and conditions.
THE IDEA (the chat's own, from putting today's results together): prediction alone does not pay - session 7's conditions pick the right moment 69% of the time on unseen days yet lose as trades, because a taker pays the gap twice. Offering alone risks being filled only when the price is about to move against us. Put them together: use the prediction only to choose WHICH SIDE to offer and WHEN TO STAND ASIDE. A quoter that is right 55-69% of the time about the next move, and collects the gap instead of paying it, is the business. Test exactly that.
PART 1 - STEERED QUOTING ON BETFAIR, before the start (racing first, then football, tennis): at each quoting moment compute session 7's condition scores from what is known then (first-half-fitted only). Variants: (a) offer only on the side the prediction favours (back when the price is expected to shorten, the lay only under 3.0); (b) offer both sides but pull the side the prediction is against; (c) stand aside entirely when the prediction is strong against both or the market is jumpy (define from first-half data); (d) unsteered, from session 10, as the control. Same sizes, stops, time limits and flat-before-the-off rule as session 10. Judge on the second half and on 20-21 Nov, under both fill models. Report: round trips, one-sided fills, what the steer changes about the price 30 s and 2 min after a fill (the adverse-move measure), £ per £100 turned over, £ a day at £10/£25/£50, worst day, result without the best day.
PART 2 - THIN MARKETS PRICED FROM DEEP ONES: for football, build each minute a fair price for every correct score, half-time result, both-teams-to-score, over/under line other than 2.5, draw no bet and double chance FROM the deep markets of the same match at that moment (match odds and over/under 2.5; a standard goals model fitted to those two markets' prices - check its fit on the first half of dates and state the error). Then (a) measure how wide the gap between back and lay is in each thin market and how far the mid sits from our fair price; (b) simulate offering BACK orders above fair and lays (under 3.0 only) below fair by a margin (1%, 2%, 3%, 5% of price), small size, repriced as the deep markets move, closed by an opposite offer or held to the result as separate variants (the held one labelled BET, with its worst day); fills by the same two models. Where trades in a thin market are too rare to model fills, say so and give the count. Same for racing place markets against win markets if both are recorded.
PART 3 - STEERED BY THE GOAL PREDICTOR, in play, only if goal_trade.md says the predictor has uplift on unseen dates: quote overs/unders with the steer, minimum order life respecting Betfair's in-play delay (model a 5-second delay on our orders and say what it costs), stop on the opposite event.
VERDICT, first line of /root/pe-queue/reports/steered_quoting.md (plain English, one screen): does steering turn offering into profit on unseen days under BOTH fill models - YES / NOT YET / NO - with £ a day at £25 and the number of markets a day. Line 2: thin markets from deep ones, same form. Line 3: what is now on paper. If YES anywhere it goes on paper as a live quoter (forward only). Then tables. Commit and push: cd /root/pe-queue && git add reports/steered_quoting.md && git commit -qm "steered quoting; thin markets from deep ones" && git pull -q --no-rebase --no-edit && env -u GITHUB_TOKEN git push -q. Then end the session.
BRIEF
cat > $B/run_builder_steer.sh <<'SH'
